\description{
This function grafts missing taxa onto an ultrametric, time-calibrated backbone phylogeny.
The position of each taxon is constrained by its taxonomic assignment (the target clade, defined in \code{Target_clade}),
and optionally by taxonomic exclusions (the excluded subclades, defined in \code{Excluded_subclades}),
and/or exclusions based on species attributes (the allowed states, defined in \code{allowed_states} based on the \code{simmap}).

The divergence times of the grafted branches are either drawn uniformly along the branches (\code{method = "uniform"}),
or drawn from a birth-death model fitted on the target clade (\code{method = "BD_clades"}, default).
The backbone is never modified: only new tips are added.
}
\details{
\subsection{Stop conditions}{

The function stops, before any grafting, if the backbone is not ultrametric, or is not binary (i.e., all nodes have only two descendant lineages).
It also stops if some taxa have no branch available for grafting. The taxa concerned are listed in the error message,
so the constraints (\code{include_target_clade_stem}, \code{Excluded_subclades}, \code{exclude_subclades_stem} or, with a \code{simmap}, the allowed states) can be adjusted.
With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the function can also stop during grafting if no usable divergence age is found after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws (see 'Methods').
}

\subsection{Target clades, stem regions and excluded subclades}{

Each taxon is grafted within its target clade. The branches available for grafting are all the branches of the target clade
(descending from its MRCA node), plus its stem region if the stem is available for this taxon (\code{missing_taxa_list$include_target_clade_stem} of the taxon if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{include_target_clade_stem}).
Grafting on the stem places the taxon sister to the target clade.
Taxa of the same target clade can therefore differ in their stem availability: the clade is the same (same MRCA node, same richness information),
but the branches and the time window available for the divergence times of these taxa differ.

The stem region is not restricted to the original stem branch. A taxon grafted on the stem splits it in two pieces, and becomes itself part of the stem region:
later taxa can be grafted on both pieces of the stem, and on the terminal branch of the earlier taxon (the two taxa are then sister, and together sister to the target clade).
More generally, the stem region includes all the branches of the 'missing-only subtrees' hanging from the stem, i.e., subtrees containing only taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list$Taxon_name},
whatever their own target clade. This mirrors what happens within the crown group, where missing taxa can already be grafted on the branches of other missing taxa.

Branches inside excluded subclades are removed from the pool of available branches. By default (\code{exclude_subclades_stem = TRUE}),
the stem region of the excluded subclades is removed as well: the taxon can be neither nested within the excluded subclade,
nor sister to it (nor sister to the missing taxa already grafted on its stem).
This is the only way to exclude a single tip, as its terminal branch is its only branch.
With \code{exclude_subclades_stem = FALSE} (globally, or for a given subclade in \code{missing_taxa_list$exclude_subclades_stem}), the stem of the excluded subclade stays available:
the taxon can be sister to the excluded subclade, but never nested within it. A single tip then excludes nothing.
The setting is specific to each excluded subclade, so a taxon can have excluded subclades with different settings.

Excluded subclades are expected to be nested within their target clade. The function checks their position before grafting:
a warning is issued if an excluded subclade is outside the target clade (the exclusion then has no effect).
The function stops if an excluded subclade contains the whole target clade, or is identical to it: no branch (or only the stem branch) would be left for grafting.
To graft taxa specifically on the stem branch of a clade or of a single tip, use \code{\link[=graft_missing_clades]{phylograftR::graft_missing_clades()}} instead.
A warning lists the single-tip excluded subclades with a stem that is not excluded, as they exclude nothing.
}

\subsection{Enclosing clades}{

Under the \code{"BD_clades"} method, when a target clade contains fewer tips than \code{min_tips}, an alternative higher clade is selected to fit the birth-death model.
This 'enclosing clade' is identified by walking rootward the tree node by node, starting from the target clade, until a clade meets the \code{min_tips} condition.
This ensures that the diversification dynamics modeled are based on enough tips to be relevant, while being closely associated with the target clade.
}

\subsection{Methods}{
\itemize{
\item \code{"uniform"}: Taxa are grafted one by one, per target clade from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
Within each target clade, taxa are grafted in random order. The branch used for grafting is drawn among the available branches
with probabilities proportional to branch lengths, and the position on the branch is drawn from a uniform distribution.
This is fast, but does not account for diversification dynamics.
Note that under a birth-death process, the probability of a side lineage splitting off a branch at age t follows the exponential function e^(-rt),
with r the net diversification rate (speciation minus extinction).
A uniform draw implicitly assumes a flat density (r = 0), and gives too much weight to old divergence times compared to the expectation from a birth-death process.
\item \code{"BD_clades"}: Taxa are grafted per target clade, from the smallest to the largest, so nested clades are processed first (see below).
For each target clade, a birth-death model is fitted with \code{phytools::fit.bd()} on the clade, or on the smallest enclosing clade with at least \code{min_tips} tips.
The sampling fraction accounts for the number of missing taxa provided for grafting in this clade.
The divergence times of all taxa missing in the target clade are then jointly drawn with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}, between the present and the stem age of the clade
(or its crown age if the stem is not available for these taxa).
Taxa are grafted from the oldest to the youngest divergence time following this procedure:
\enumerate{
\item A taxon is grafted at its age if an available branch in an allowed state crosses this age in the current tree.
\item The ages for which no such valid branch piece exists are discarded.
\item The ages of the taxa left are drawn again jointly, and grafted or discarded in the same way.
This is repeated until all the taxa of the group are grafted, up to \code{max_age_redraws} times.
Taxa of the same target clade with different stem availability (or, with a \code{simmap}, different allowed states) are drawn in separate groups,
and the birth-death model is fitted only once per target clade.
\code{"BD_clades"} is the recommended grafting method, but it can be time consuming for large phylogenies.
}
}

With both methods, nested clades are processed first, so the taxa of a larger clade are grafted on a tree that already
includes the taxa of the clades nested within it, and can be grafted on their branches.
For taxa of the same clade, those without access to the stem come first.
For \code{"BD_clades"}, it also ensures that the divergence times of nested clades are accounted for when drawing the divergence times of the larger clade.
Taxa of the same target clade are grafted in random order, so they are exchangeable.
}

\subsection{Species attributes: allowed states of a simmap}{

A \code{simmap} describes the states of a discrete attribute (e.g., a trait or a range) along the branches of the backbone: each branch is cut in pieces, each piece being in one state.
With a \code{simmap}, each taxon can only be grafted on the pieces of the available branches that are in its allowed states
(\code{missing_taxa_list$allowed_states} of the taxon if provided, otherwise the global argument \code{allowed_states}).
This combines with the other constraints: the available branches are those of the target clade, minus the excluded subclades, restricted to the allowed states.
If all states are allowed (all states listed in \code{allowed_states}, or no allowed states at all), grafting is not restricted by the states,
and constraints are only based on target clades and excluded subclades.

A grafted taxon is assumed to be in the state of the point of the backbone where it was grafted, along its whole branch,
and so are the taxa grafted on its branch later. Taxa grafted later can therefore be grafted on the branches of taxa grafted earlier,
if their state is allowed.

The states are updated alongside the phylogeny, and the updated \code{simmap} is returned in \verb{$simmap} if \code{return_simmap = TRUE}.
A \code{simmap} can thus be provided with \code{allowed_states = NULL}, only to get the states of the grafted taxa and branches:
grafting is then not restricted by the states and gives the same result as without \code{simmap} (for the same \code{seed}).

With \code{method = "uniform"}, the draw is uniform over the available pieces (i.e., portion of branches in the allowed states), weighted by their length:
every time point in an allowed state has the same chance to be selected.

With \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the divergence ages of the taxa of a group (same target clade, same stem availability and same allowed states) are drawn jointly
with \code{TreeSim::corsim()}, as without a \code{simmap}: the diversification model is not modified by the states.
The same grafting procedure, described above without a \code{simmap}, is then used except that
an age can only be used if an available branch in an allowed state crosses it (without a \code{simmap}, if an available branch crosses it).
The ages are used from the oldest to the youngest, each for one taxon of the group.
}

\subsection{Biogeographic ranges as states}{

With \code{treat_states_as_ranges = TRUE}, the states of the \code{simmap} are biogeographic ranges, coded as in \code{BioGeoBEARS}
(one capital letter per area, and the letters of a multi-area range in alphabetical order, e.g., \code{"AB"}),
and the allowed states are the ranges a missing clade can have.

An allowed range matches a state of the \code{simmap} if it is contained in it: a missing taxon allowed in \code{"A"} can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"A"}, \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"},
whereas a missing taxon allowed in \code{"AB"} only can be grafted on branch pieces in \code{"AB"} or \code{"ABC"}.

When multiple allowed states are provided, the inherited range is drawn randomly from the allowed ranges enclosed within the range of the branch piece where it is grafted.
When grafting on an edge with a larger range than the inherited range (e.g., \code{"A"} on \code{"AB"}), this results in a subset sympatric speciation event such as (AB) -> (AB), (A).
In the returned \code{simmap}, the branches of the grafted lineage are in their inherited range, whereas the branch it is grafted on continues in its own range.
}

\subsection{Multiple simulations}{

With \code{nb_simulations > 1}, the grafting is simulated \code{nb_simulations} times from the same backbone and the same \code{missing_taxa_list}.
Each simulation starts again from the backbone: the birth-death models are fitted on the tree of the simulation (they depend on the taxa already grafted),
and the simulations do not share anything but the inputs. The phylogenies are returned in a \code{"multiPhylo"} object (a single \code{"phylo"} if \code{nb_simulations = 1}),
and the memory used grows with the number of simulations.

The first simulation uses \code{seed}, so it is the same phylogeny as a run with \code{nb_simulations = 1} and the same \code{seed}.
The seeds of the other simulations are drawn from a stream started by \code{seed}, and returned in the attribute \code{"seeds"}:
a simulation can be reproduced alone with its seed, and the first simulations of a longer run are the same as the ones of a shorter run.
With \code{seed = NULL}, the simulations follow the random number generator of the session.

A failure that is specific to a simulation, because of the random draws (no usable divergence age or time after \code{max_age_redraws} redraws), skips this simulation with a warning:
the name of a phylogeny keeps the index of its simulation (e.g., \code{"sim_3"} is missing if the third simulation failed). The function reports the number of phylogenies obtained
if it differs from \code{nb_simulations}, and stops if no simulation succeeded. With \code{force_simulations = TRUE}, new seeds are tried until \code{nb_simulations} phylogenies are obtained
(up to \code{10 * nb_simulations} attempts): the phylogenies are then named \code{"sim_1"} to \code{"sim_N"} without gaps, and the seeds of the failed attempts are in the attribute \code{"failed"}.
Any other error (invalid inputs, no branch available for a taxon, ...) concerns all the simulations and stops the whole run.

With \code{save_temp = TRUE} and several simulations, each simulation saves its temporary phylogeny in its own file, with the suffix \code{"_sim_k"} added before \code{".rds"} in \code{temp_file_path}.
}

\subsection{Multiple stochastic maps}{

When multiple stochastic maps of the same trait are provided as a \code{"multiSimmap"}, the grafting is run with each of its maps.
The maps are alternative hypotheses of the evolution of the same trait (or range), used as alternative constraints.
All the maps and the simulations are crossed: each map is used for \code{nb_simulations} simulations, so \code{P} maps give \code{P * nb_simulations} grafted phylogenies
This can lead to a quick inflation of computation time, thus a warning is sent if the total number of grafted phylogenies (\code{P * nb_simulations}) is more than 1000.

The output is a list with one element per stochastic map, named after the maps (the names of the \code{"multiSimmap"} if it has some, otherwise \code{"map_1"}, ..., \code{"map_P"}).
With \code{nb_simulations = 1}, each element is a grafted phylogeny. With several simulations, each element is a \code{"multiPhylo"} (see 'Multiple simulations')
with phylogenies named \code{"<map>_sim_1"}, \code{"<map>_sim_2"}, ... and the attributes \code{"seeds"} and \code{"failed"} of the map.
Each phylogeny can be reproduced alone with its map (as a single \code{simmap}) and its seed.

A map for which no simulation could be obtained is dropped from the output with a warning, and listed in the attribute \code{"failed_maps"} of the list
(data.frame with the columns \code{map} and \code{reason}). The function stops if no map gives any phylogeny.
With \code{save_temp = TRUE}, the temporary files get the suffix \code{"_map_<name>"} before the suffix of the simulation.
}

\subsection{Sampling fraction and unsampled species}{

Under \code{method = "BD_clades"}, the birth-death model of a clade is fitted with the sampling fraction
\code{n_present / (n_present + n_pending + n_unsampled)}, with \code{n_present} the tips of the fitted clade in the current tree (backbone tips and taxa already grafted),
\code{n_pending} the taxa of \code{missing_taxa_list} still to graft in this clade, and \code{n_unsampled} the unsampled species derived from \code{total_richness_per_clades}.
Taxa grafted in nested clades processed earlier are already in the tree, so they are not counted as missing anymore.
Unsampled species are only used to fit the rates: they are not grafted, and do not compete with the grafted taxa for the branches.

For a clade with a \code{total_richness} value provided: \code{nb_unsampled = total_richness - nb_backbone - nb_missing}, with \code{nb_missing} the number of taxa to graft
whose target clade is this clade or is nested within it. For a clade with no \code{total_richness} value (\code{NA}, or enclosing a target clade without being listed),
\code{nb_unsampled} is the sum over the largest clades nested within it with a \code{total_richness} value provided.
As such, the unsampled species of nested clades are summed across disjoint clades but never counted twice within nested clades.

If \code{total_richness_per_clades = NULL}, \code{nb_unsampled = 0} and no \code{sampling_fraction_df} is returned.
To obtain the \code{sampling_fraction_df} without informing any total richness, provide \code{total_richness_per_clades} with the clades and only \code{NA} in \verb{$total_richness}:
\code{nb_unsampled = 0} and \code{total_richness} equals the backbone plus missing species in all clades (the sampling fraction is 1).

The function stops, before any grafting, if \code{total_richness_per_clades} does not cover all the target clades, if a \code{total_richness} provided is lower than
the number of backbone species plus species to graft of the clade, or if a clade has fewer unsampled species than the sum of the clades with
a \code{total_richness} provided nested within it.
With \code{method = "uniform"}, no diversification model is used: \code{total_richness_per_clades} is only used to derive the \code{sampling_fraction_df} table (a warning is issued).
}
}
\examples{
# --- Prepare data --- #

# Create a toy backbone phylogeny: ultrametric, with a root age of 50 time units
set.seed(1234)
phy <- ape::rcoal(n = 30)
phy$edge.length <- phy$edge.length / max(ape::node.depth.edgelength(phy)) * 50

# List the missing taxa to graft
missing_taxa_list <- list(
  Taxon_name = c("X1", "X2", "X3", "X4"),
  # Target clades defined by pairs of tips (MRCA), or by a single tip
  # All the other items are named after Taxon_name (same names, same order)
  Target_clade = list(X1 = c("t26", "t18"), X2 = c("t8", "t22"), X3 = "t5", X4 = c("t26", "t18")),
  # Stem of the target clade available for grafting?
  # NA uses the global 'include_target_clade_stem' argument (TRUE by default)
  # X4 cannot be grafted on the stem
  include_target_clade_stem = c(X1 = NA, X2 = NA, X3 = NA, X4 = FALSE),
  # Excluded subclades defined by pairs of tips. Only X1 has an excluded subclade
  # (the optional name of the excluded subclade, here 'clade_A', is used in messages)
  Excluded_subclades = list(X1 = list(clade_A = c("t10", "t19")), X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL),
  # Stem of the excluded subclades also excluded?
  # NA / NULL use the global 'exclude_subclades_stem' argument (TRUE by default).
  # Here the stem of the excluded subclade of X1 stays available,
  # so X1 can be sister to it, but never nested within it.
  exclude_subclades_stem = list(X1 = FALSE, X2 = NULL, X3 = NULL, X4 = NULL))

# --- Perform grafting --- #

# Graft with the default method ("BD_clades")
grafted_phy <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                  method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7)

# Color the grafted tips and branches in red based on elements returned with the phylogeny
plot(grafted_phy, cex = 0.7,
     edge.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy$grafted_edges + 1],
     tip.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy$grafted_tips + 1])

# Graft with the "uniform" method, without using the excluded subclades
grafted_phy_uniform <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                          use_excluded_subclades = FALSE,
                                          method = "uniform", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Check that the constraints are respected
check_grafting_constraints(phy_grafted = grafted_phy, phy_backbone = phy,
                           missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list)

# --- Produce several grafted phylogenies --- #

# The grafting is stochastic: several simulations can be run at once from the same backbone and list
# (a "multiPhylo" named sim_1, sim_2, ...; sim_1 is the same as a single run with the same seed)
grafted_phys <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                    method = "uniform", nb_simulations = 3, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
names(grafted_phys)
# Seeds of the simulations, to reproduce each phylogeny alone
attr(grafted_phys, "seeds")

# Plot phylos
old_par <- par()["mfrow"]
par(mfrow = c(1, 3))
plot(grafted_phys[[1]], cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #1") # sim_1
plot(grafted_phys[[2]], cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #2") # sim_2
plot(grafted_phys[[3]], cex = 0.7, main = "Sim #3") # sim_3
par(old_par)

# --- Account for unsampled species --- #

# Account for unsampled species with the total richness of the clades.
# All the unique target clades must be listed
# (NA = unknown richness, i.e., no unsampled species beyond nested clades)
total_richness_per_clades <- list(
  Target_clade = list(c("t26", "t18"), c("t8", "t22"), "t5"),
  total_richness = c(25, NA, 3))
grafted_phy_richness <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = phy, missing_taxa_list = missing_taxa_list,
                                           total_richness_per_clades = total_richness_per_clades,
                                           method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)
# Sampling fraction of the clades in the grafted phylogeny
grafted_phy_richness$sampling_fraction_df

# --- Apply attribute-based exclusions --- #

# Restrict the grafting to some states of a stochastic character map (simmap) of the backbone
# The toy simmap has three states, "blue", "green" and "red"
data(toy_backbone)
data(toy_simmap)
data(toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states)

# This missing_taxa_list has the item 'allowed_states', with the states where each taxon can be grafted
# (NULL for the taxa without a specific value).
toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states$allowed_states

# Perform grafting informed with the simmap and associated 'allowed_states'.
# The taxa without taxon-level states use the global 'allowed_states' argument:
# here they can only be grafted on "blue" or "green" sections of the branches
grafted_phy_states <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
                                         missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states,
                                         simmap = toy_simmap, allowed_states = c("blue", "green"),
                                         method = "BD_clades", verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Plot output phylogeny with colored grafts
plot(grafted_phy_states, cex = 0.7,
     edge.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy_states$grafted_edges + 1],
     tip.color = c("black", "red")[grafted_phy_states$grafted_tips + 1])

# The updated simmap is in the output: the grafted taxa are in the state of the point where they were grafted
plot(grafted_phy_states$simmap, fsize = 0.5, ftype = "i",
     colors = c("blue" = "dodgerblue", "green" = "limegreen", "red" = "firebrick"))

# States of the grafted taxa: "Genus_a_sp1" and "Genus_c_sp2" can be "red" thanks to their taxon-level states,
# but the taxa using the global states cannot
phytools::getStates(grafted_phy_states$simmap, type = "tips")[toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states$Taxon_name]


# --- Use several stochastic maps (alternative hypotheses of trait evolution) as attribute-based constraints --- #

# A 'multiSimmap' gives several stochastic maps of the same trait: the grafting is run with each of them.
# Here, 3 maps are simulated from the states of the tips of the toy simmap.
tip_states <- phytools::getStates(toy_simmap, type = "tips")

# (the random number generator of the session is restored afterwards)
had_seed <- exists(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv(), inherits = FALSE)
if (had_seed) old_seed <- get(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv(), inherits = FALSE)
set.seed(118)
toy_multiSimmap <- phytools::make.simmap(toy_backbone, tip_states, model = "ER", nsim = 3, message = FALSE)
if (had_seed) assign(".Random.seed", old_seed, envir = globalenv()) else rm(".Random.seed", envir = globalenv())

# Graft using constraints from all 3 simmaps X 2 simulations per simmap
grafted_phys_maps <- graft_missing_taxa(phy = toy_backbone,
                                        missing_taxa_list = toy_missing_taxa_list_with_allowed_states,
                                        simmap = toy_multiSimmap, allowed_states = c("blue", "green"),
                                        method = "BD_clades", nb_simulations = 2, verbose = FALSE, seed = 1234)

# Inspect output:
# A list with one element per simmap, each a multiPhylo with the simulations of the map
names(grafted_phys_maps)
names(grafted_phys_maps$map_2)
# The map and the seed of a phylogeny
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "map")
attr(grafted_phys_maps$map_2[[1]], "seed")


}
\seealso{
Other functions from phylograftR:
\itemize{
